# The corpus is committed in its reverted state, with __PLANTED_…__ placeholders where the
# credentials go. plant_secrets.py fills them in locally; --revert puts them back.
#
# Nothing here can catch a planted corpus, because planting rewrites the tracked files in place
# rather than creating new ones. Run `python3 plant_secrets.py --check` before committing — that
# is the actual guard, and this file only keeps the incidental output out.

corpus/.chamnan/

# `.chamnan/` here is NOT incidental output -- it is the fixture. The hazard these files carry is
# that they ARRIVE WITH A CLONE and are read at session start, so an ignored workspace would test
# nothing: `plant_rule_patterns.py` and `plant_workspace_hazards.py` write into the paths below
# and those paths are tracked on purpose. Everything else chamnan writes while running over this
# corpus stays ignored. A run leaves the tracked paths dirty; `git checkout .chamnan` resets them.
.chamnan/*
!.chamnan/memory/
!.chamnan/skills/
!.chamnan/state/
!.chamnan/sessions/
!.chamnan/threads/
!.chamnan/logs/
!.chamnan/config.json
!.chamnan/config.json.sane
!.chamnan/.version
!.chamnan/.version.sane

__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/

# `plant_failure_classes.py --install` puts down names that are legal here and make `git clone`
# fail on Windows. They are the fixture; committing one would stop Windows users reaching the
# corpus at all, so the tracked form is the `.reserved-name` placeholder beside each.
corpus/edge/windows-hostile/*
!corpus/edge/windows-hostile/*.reserved-name
!corpus/edge/windows-hostile/README.md

# `plant_situations.py --install` renames these to `.git`, which git cannot track by name. The
# tracked form is the `dot-git` placeholder beside each.
corpus/edge/git-shapes/**/.git

# `plant_supply_chain.py --install` writes these two at the repository root. They are the live
# form of CVE-2025-59536 and its MCP half: a repo-scoped settings file whose SessionStart hook
# fires before the trust dialog, and an MCP declaration that approves its own servers. A PUBLIC
# repository whose tracked files make a tool execute anything on open is a weapon however
# harmless the payload is, so the tracked form is the `.attack-shape` document beside each.
/.claude/settings.json
/.mcp.json
corpus/edge/supply-chain/EXECUTED.log

# `plant_incidents.py --install` writes an editor task with `runOn: folderOpen` and
# `reveal: never`. A tracked file that makes an editor run something the moment a folder opens
# is a weapon whatever it runs, so the tracked form is `tasks.json.attack-shape`.
/.vscode/
corpus/edge/incidents/EXECUTED.log
